      "content": "Migrating legacy C codebases to Rust promises memory safety, but translating complex systems usually relies on bounded model checking or test suites that leave corner cases completely uncovered. Translators, whether rule-based or powered by large language models, lack formal guarantees that program behavior remains strictly identical.\n\nA new framework called R2C2 introduces formal translation validation at the LLVM Intermediate Representation level. Instead of trying to formalize surface syntax across disparate languages, it maps both C and Rust to LLVM IR and establishes a refinement relation in the Rocq proof assistant.\n\nTo bridge diverging memory representations, the authors introduce shared abstract value semantics for library calls. Crucially, they automate the construction of program-specific refinement proofs using AI agents while offloading verification to Rocq, eliminating hallucinations from the final guarantee.\n\nRigorous semantic equivalence checking without manual theorem proving makes automated systems modernization genuinely dependable."
