      "content": "Most engineering teams evaluating coding agents look purely at benchmark pass rates. However, understanding the physical economics of autonomous code generation requires looking directly at where tokens actually disappear.\n\nA recent paper applies principles of stochastic thermodynamics to agentic coding cycles, treating the agent cost as energy and repository test suites as validation daemons. Across repeated coding and uncoding cycles, the researchers discovered that approximately 80 percent of output tokens generated during successful runs produce zero permanent changes to the target codebase.\n\nThese discarded tokens represent computational heat rather than useful mechanical work. The authors found that the free energy of an intentional code change constitutes merely 14 to 22 percent of the total cost, and coding typically requires 1.1 to 1.5 times more energy than cleanly reverting edits.\n\nOptimizing agent systems demands tighter harness constraints and aggressive context trimming rather than simply throwing longer generation windows at complex tasks."
