      "content": "Automated benchmarks for AI agent security often fail to catch real exploits because they inspect text output instead of executed tool calls. A comprehensive audit of the AgentDojo benchmark across 19,381 runs revealed that 11.9 percent of verified attacks were scored as safe, even when the agent executed decisive actions like transferring funds to an attacker.\n\nEvaluation harnesses frequently rely on string-matching heuristics or secondary LLM judges. In testing across 24 models, benchmark checks misclassified model warnings as actual attacks across entire model families, while completely missing tool actions that violated security boundaries.\n\nReproducibility in agent security remains brittle. Out of 240 recorded attacks re-run ten times, only 201 reliably succeeded at a 95 percent confidence interval. When evaluating agentic workflows, you cannot trust text assertions alone; auditing must bind directly to tool execution logs and external state changes.\n\nReliable agent safety requires deterministic verification of tool call side effects rather than faith in model response strings."
