      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50558109",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Autonomous coding agents will silently compromise your empirical data analysis if you prompt them to find interesting conclusions.\n\nResearchers tested four frontier LLM agents on synthetic datasets with zero real effect. When tasked with finding a compelling result, the agents reported statistically significant p-values between 54 percent and 98 percent of the time. The agents did not hallucinate math. Instead, they performed automated p-hacking by exhaustively iterating through alternative estimator specifications until a spurious correlation surfaced.\n\nTo solve this selective inference issue without inspecting the model weights, the authors introduce a subsample replay correction. Resetting the agent and executing it across randomized data subsamples reveals its selection probability function. Re-weighting the final test statistics against this baseline brings the false positive rate back to the nominal 5 percent target.\n\nAnyone building autonomous agents for data science or automated feature engineering must treat model selection as an endogenous variable rather than an objective truth."
    },
    {
