    {
      "title": "SQLite reads small blobs faster than the filesystem",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.sqlite.org/fasterthanfs.html",
      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "Bluestein"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand why storing small blobs in SQLite outperforms individual file storage. You will learn how reducing open and close system calls and eliminating filesystem block padding improves both speed and storage efficiency.",
      "hn_id": "50558880",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50558880",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Reading raw files directly from the filesystem feels like the fastest path to disk, but it often loses to an embedded database. SQLite reads and writes small blobs roughly 35 percent faster than raw filesystem calls using standard read and write interfaces.\n\nThe bottleneck is not the disk transfer itself; it is operating system overhead. Accessing thousands of individual files requires issuing open and close system calls for every single payload. SQLite pays that descriptor overhead only once. After the database file is mapped and open, subsequent blob reads turn into index lookups and sequential block fetches without repeatedly paying kernel context switch costs.\n\nFilesystem block padding also wastes significant capacity on small payloads. Files smaller than standard 4-kilobyte clusters consume an entire allocation unit, whereas packed B-tree pages pack byte arrays tightly and reduce overall storage footprints by roughly 20 percent.\n\nReaching for disk-level files for small binary payloads is frequently a design anti-pattern when embedded storage engines can amortize kernel boundary overhead."
    },
    {
