      "image": null,
      "content": "Running local LLM inference on Apple Silicon has long suffered from unexpected latency spikes and memory overhead caused by CPU-GPU synchronization bottlenecks. PyTorch 2.15 introduces fundamental architectural overhauls to the Metal Performance Shaders backend that directly tackle these pipeline stalls.\n\nThe biggest win comes from native grouped matrix multiplication and specialized decoding paths for Mixture of Experts models. Decoding speeds for architectures like Qwen3.6-35B jump from 9.4 to 61.3 tokens per second on an M3 Ultra. By replacing high-overhead MPSGraph abstractions with dedicated Metal kernels and optimizing top-k expert routing, the runtime eliminates costly host synchronizations.\n\nIn addition, native FP8 support arrives with float8_e4m3fn datatypes, cutting memory footprints in half compared to BF16 while unlocking scaled matrix multiplication. Single-row linear layers now leverage dedicated matrix-vector kernels, and vocabulary softmax operations run eight times faster.\n\nLocal hardware acceleration is shifting rapidly from experimental emulation to first-class production compute."
    },
    {
      "title": "Switching LLMs mid-conversation is a ski-rental problem",
