      "authors": [
        "Khaled Maâmra"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand why dynamically switching models during long multi-turn conversations requires balancing cache rebuild overhead against inference savings. It frames the caching tradeoff as an online ski-rental problem with practical routing strategies.",
      "hn_id": "50561397",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50561397",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most LLM routing strategies evaluate requests in isolation: a classifier routes simple queries to small models and complex queries to frontier models. In multi-turn agentic workflows, this naive routing breaks down because it ignores prompt caching.\n\nEvery time a router switches models mid-session, the downstream provider cannot reuse the established prefix cache. Rebuilding that context history on a new model incurs an immediate latency and token penalty that often wipes out the cost savings of using a cheaper model.\n\nTreating model switching as an online ski-rental problem provides a formal mathematical framework for this trade-off. Instead of switching immediately when task difficulty drops, the router waits until the cumulative savings of the cheaper model exceed the one-time switching cost of cache invalidation.\n\nEvaluating routing policies against historical trace schedules allows teams to optimize both latency and infrastructure spend without thrashing model context."
    },
    {
      "title": "Higher traffic reduces Postgres read replica replication lag",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://clickhouse.com/blog/tuning-postgres-read-replicas",
      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "Colton Shepard"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand the counterintuitive relationship between workload and Postgres replication lag, and learn practical configuration tweaks to keep replicas tightly synchronized.",
      "hn_id": "50558991",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50558991",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Conventional database wisdom assumes heavier write loads increase replication delay on read replicas. In PostgreSQL, the exact opposite often occurs: low-traffic primaries experience far higher perceived replica lag than busy ones.\n\nThe culprit lies in WAL flush mechanics. When write traffic is low, PostgreSQL does not fill 16 megabyte WAL segments quickly. If your streaming configuration falls back to segment archiving, replica updates stall waiting for WAL chunks to flush, creating artificial latency spikes on quiet systems.\n\nSetting archive_timeout to 5 seconds and dialing wal_retrieve_retry_interval down to 500 milliseconds forces frequent flushes of partial WAL files. This keeps standby nodes tightly synchronized without needing synthetic write injection, though you must account for the increased network and storage I/O.\n\nCounterintuitive replication latency is almost always a batching artifact rather than an engine bottleneck."
    },
