      "content": "Most discussions around AI agents focus on autonomous multi-hour workflows, yet production reality looks completely different. In a systematic study of eighty-six production agent deployments across twenty-six domains, researchers found that sixty-eight percent of production agents execute ten or fewer steps before requiring human intervention.\n\nFurthermore, seventy percent of engineering teams rely entirely on prompting off-the-shelf models rather than fine-tuning weights. Reliability remains the single largest operational roadblock. Instead of relying on model-level improvements, successful teams solve reliability almost exclusively through systems-level architecture, deterministic guardrails, and structured verification stages.\n\nIf you are designing agentic software today, do not overcomplicate your harness with open-ended autonomy. Restrict the state space, enforce short execution horizons, and let deterministic software handle the guardrails."
