      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "khalidkhair"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how different language runtimes interpret Linux container CPU quotas versus core affinity. You will learn how these mismatched calculations impact worker thread sizing and performance.",
      "hn_id": "50567644",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50567644",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Container CPU limits will mislead your application runtimes if you rely on standard concurrency APIs. When a Linux cgroup specifies five eligible CPUs in its cpuset but caps total compute via cpu.max to four cores of execution time, modern runtimes split into two opposing camps.\n\nPython 3.13 and Go 1.23 inspect affinity masks and report five available CPUs. OpenJDK 21 and Node.js 22 inspect cgroup bandwidth quotas and report four. This discrepancy directly impacts thread pool sizing, worker count, and runtime scheduling. If a process spins up five CPU-heavy threads under a four-core quota, the Linux Completely Fair Scheduler throttles the entire container once its multi-thread burst exhausts the quota period.\n\nFor backend services running in Kubernetes, oversubscribing workers causes tail latency spikes due to CFS quota throttling rather than actual CPU starvation. You cannot safely trust runtime defaults to discover your real compute boundaries.\n\nAlways align your runtime thread pool configurations with actual cgroup bandwidth quotas rather than logical processor affinity."
    },
    {
