      "content": "Prompt caching saves significant latency and inference spend, but the eviction mechanics remain a trap for developer tooling. When using interactive coding agents, developers rarely submit consecutive prompts within ten seconds. They run tests, inspect diffs, and think.\n\nInvestigating why weekly quotas vanished revealed that prompt cache time-to-live expiration was the real culprit. If a developer pauses for five minutes, the entire conversation context must be re-ingested at full token cost on the next prompt.\n\nDisassembling the underlying client binary uncovered a dormant keep-alive subsystem designed to touch the cache. By testing cache invalidation windows down to fifteen-second resolution, the author demonstrated how minimal background pings keep prompt cache state hot.\n\nUnderstanding your provider prompt cache eviction thresholds is essential for designing cost-efficient interactive agent loops."
