      "content": "Serving agent workloads breaks conventional LLM inference schedulers because agent execution patterns fluctuate wildly between massive prompt ingestion, multi-turn reasoning, and frequent tool invocation. Traditional systems like vLLM rely on static offline profiling to balance prefill and decode stages, which degrades throughput when interactive agent traffic shifts abruptly.\n\nMIT HAN Lab designed Evolving Infra to eliminate offline calibration completely by decoupling compute allocation from key-value cache management. The system monitors live request patterns and shifts individual nodes between prefill and decode roles on the fly without invalidating or migrating cached states across the cluster.\n\nEvaluations using complex agent benchmarks like OSWorld and Putnam show up to a 4.1 times reduction in end-to-end latency and a 14 times reduction in 95th percentile time to first token over existing serving architectures on modern GPU clusters.\n\nDecoupling cache locality from compute scheduling is rapidly becoming the essential architecture for scalable agent infrastructure."
