    {
      "title": "ZFS provides the foundational storage primitives for persistent coding agents",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://ingresslabs.net/blog/zfs-for-agent-workloads",
      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "IngressLabs"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how ZFS datasets, snapshots, and copy-on-write trees uniquely solve the isolation and checkpointing demands of autonomous coding agents.",
      "hn_id": "50566128",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50566128",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Building persistent sandboxes for coding agents reveals that the primary infrastructure bottleneck is not compute, but disk management. Most platforms stitch together container overlays, background snapshot daemons, and custom diffing tools to isolate and rewind execution states, creating substantial latency and operational complexity.\n\nZFS solves this entire lifecycle at the storage engine level through its unified dataset abstraction. Because ZFS uses a copy-on-write tree structure where every write is allocated to a new block, taking a checkpoint merely requires pinning an existing Merkle tree root. Forking an isolated, writable session becomes an instant clone operation that shares existing storage until mutations occur.\n\nExposing datasets as block volumes through virtio-blk allows microVMs to boot instantly against verified root states. Storage accounting and rollbacks operate as native primitives rather than application-layer hacks, accurately tracking allocated space down to individual block allocations.\n\nWhen infrastructure requirements demand fast state branching and instant rewinds, leaning into storage engine primitives beats reinventing them in user space."
    },
    {
