      "image": null,
      "content": "Structured output schemas force language models to waste thousands of output tokens repeating JSON keys. When generating hundreds of uniform records, emitting identical key strings across every object introduces unnecessary latency and billing overhead.\n\nWiretuple tackles this with a zero-dependency Go reverse proxy that sits between your client and upstream inference APIs. It rewrites your JSON schema to expect positional tuples with an upfront key legend, streams the compacted array over Server-Sent Events, and rehydrates the keys downstream on the fly.\n\nThis simple architectural shift cuts generation tokens by 35 to 60 percent with zero data loss. It is an elegant network-layer optimization that solves token bloat without touching application application logic."
    },
    {
      "title": "Velarx achieves fast container execution through verified storage",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://scrivano.org/posts/2026-11-18-velarx/",
      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "Giuseppe Scrivano"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how Velarx combines composefs, fs-verity, and EROFS to eliminate image unpacking overhead while enforcing an end-to-end chain of trust. You will learn how verified content-addressable storage enables instant container startup with page-level integrity checks.",
      "hn_id": "50561028",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50561028",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Traditional container engines unpack root filesystems into directories or layered tarballs, leaving a blind spot between image verification at pull time and actual file execution. Once an image is unpacked, the kernel cannot verify whether individual blocks on disk were altered before execution.\n\nVelarx rethinks this model by pairing composefs, EROFS, and fs-verity directly inside a Rust-based container runtime. Instead of extracting files, every layer payload is stored as an immutable object addressed by its fs-verity Merkle tree digest. Each layer is merely a metadata-only EROFS filesystem with overlayfs stubs pointing to content-addressed objects.\n\nBecause fs-verity verifies data page by page as the kernel reads from block storage, the chain of trust extends from the container signature directly down to kernel page cache faults. Container startup time becomes constant regardless of image size, avoiding layer unpacking or recursive permission changes.\n\nSecurity in infrastructure is strongest when integrity verification is pushed down into storage engine primitives rather than managed in user space."
    },
    {
      "title": "Minimal SQL roles reach critical native database attack surfaces",
