      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "Zheng Yu"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how low-privilege SQL roles still expose complex C and C++ database engines to severe memory corruption exploits. It reveals why logical access boundaries fail to protect against native remote code execution.",
      "hn_id": "50558941",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50558941",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Database role-based access control creates a dangerous illusion of security. Teams often assume that granting minimal privileges to a tenant or an application isolates the underlying host, but logical SQL restrictions cannot protect against memory corruption in the native execution engine.\n\nEven an unprivileged account with read-only rights or access limited to temporary tables exercises the database query optimizer, binary wire-protocol parsers, and aggregate evaluators. Because popular engines like PostgreSQL, MySQL, and Oracle are written in C and C++, any flaw in these complex execution paths turns a restricted SQL connection directly into remote code execution under the database operating system service account.\n\nRecent vulnerability research demonstrates this exact attack path across major engines, turning basic operations like temporary table creation into full host takeover. Relying strictly on logical grants without network-level isolation, sandboxing, and defense-in-depth leaves your infrastructure exposed to wide attack surfaces hidden beneath narrow permissions.\n\nLogical SQL permissions are not a sandbox for memory-unsafe native code."
    },
    {
