      "content": "Autonomous software audits are crossing the threshold from theoretical toy demos to near-saturation code vulnerability discovery. A recent case study detailed an autonomous Codex-driven security audit executed against the Webmin codebase, covering over 386,000 lines of production code.\n\nThe system discovered 507 canonical, reproduced vulnerabilities across 524 verified applicability paths, capturing an estimated 90 percent of classic flaws. The entire campaign processed roughly 8.3 billion tokens, which translates to approximately 4,500 dollars at standard API rates.\n\nWhat makes this significant for system builders is the shift from heuristic static analysis tools to autonomous agentic reasoning. The agent verified prerequisites, modeled execution paths, and generated reproducible exploits rather than emitting endless unranked alerts.\n\nThe economics of finding zero-day vulnerabilities have inverted: machine-driven automated auditing is now cheaper than human triage."
