    {
      "title": "Disabling ZFS sync accelerates writes by sacrificing durability",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://natbox.io/blog/faster-disk-writes",
      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "Mohammad AlSaraf"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how frequent fsync calls bottleneck package installations and the durability trade-offs involved in disabling synchronous writes.",
      "hn_id": "50557548",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50557548",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Every time your database commits a transaction or a package manager unpacks files, it invokes fsync and waits for the underlying storage layer to confirm. When running virtualized storage, these sync operations quickly bottleneck performance.\n\nRunning benchmarks on nested virtual disks revealed a striking drop: the host virtual disk capped forced writes at 51 operations per second. Layering ZFS on top cut throughput in half again to roughly 25 writes per second because every forced write had to be acknowledged across two distinct filesystems.\n\nTweaking logbias to favor throughput or adding loop devices with direct I/O failed to solve the physical constraint. The team ultimately bypassed the bottleneck by setting ZFS sync=disabled, boosting write speeds by over 100 times while sacrificing immediate crash consistency.\n\nHigh write throughput often hides severe durability trade-offs that every engineer must explicitly evaluate."
    },
    {
      "title": "Deleting gate weights effectively fine-tunes models without catastrophic forgetting",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://ianbarber.blog/2026/11/18/this-weight-does-not-spark-joy/",
      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "Ian"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how pruning weights in gated linear units can serve as a compact, effective fine-tuning method. You will understand how suppressing undesirable behaviors preserves base model capabilities better than traditional approaches.",
      "hn_id": "50556942",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50556942",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Fine-tuning large language models typically means modifying existing weights via full fine-tuning or adding low-rank parameter adapters with LoRA. However, a surprising post-training alternative shows that pruning weights can be far more effective.\n\nFeed-forward networks within transformer blocks rely heavily on Gated Linear Units, multiplying input transforms by gate activations. Recent research demonstrates that restricting model adaptation to simply zeroing specific gate weights creates compact masks that outperform traditional adapters.\n\nThis technique, called Deletion Tuning, produces checkpoint masks that are orders of magnitude smaller than full-parameter updates while avoiding catastrophic forgetting. By suppressing erroneous activations rather than warping established representations, the base model retains prior capabilities across tasks.\n\nPruning undesirable behaviors can often accomplish more than forcing new parameters into a trained model."
    },
    {
      "title": "Code review bottlenecks open source development despite coding agents",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/application-development/lessons-from-building-sashiko-an-agentic-code-review-system",
      "date": "2026-11-19",
      "authors": [
        "Roman Gushchin",
        "Eric Brewer"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn why the true bottleneck in open-source development is reviewing code rather than generating it, and how agentic review systems like Sashiko help maintain kernel stability under heavy patch volumes.",
